Memory probe: InMemoryCache (--cache=apollo), Node v24.21.0, darwin/arm64, median of 5 run(s), each section in a fresh process

======================================================================================
1. Retained footprint vs list breadth: store, read memo, watched query
======================================================================================

  Retained bytes, cumulative (F = 8 fields per entity)
  N       payload JSON      store     + read     + watch  per entity
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  1 000      149.6 KiB  528.0 KiB   4.67 MiB    9.05 MiB      9487 B
  5 000      778.6 KiB   3.01 MiB  23.71 MiB   46.23 MiB      9695 B
  20 000      3.13 MiB  12.51 MiB  95.78 MiB  186.65 MiB      9786 B

  Marginal cost per entity (N = 1 000 → 20 000): store 662 B, root read memo 4366 B, optimistic watch 4774 B.
  The watch adds a second memo set because optimistic reads never share the root's
  entries (performance §4.2), plus the watch's last result.

======================================================================================
2. Retained footprint by data shape: store after write, then + read
======================================================================================

  Retained bytes, cumulative
  shape                                payload JSON      store      + read
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  normalized list (2 000 entities)        306.9 KiB   1.19 MiB    9.54 MiB
  embedded list (2 000 objects)           290.4 KiB  823.5 KiB    4.34 MiB
  deep chain (D = 256)                     19.1 KiB  109.1 KiB    1.03 MiB
  nested lists (50 groups x 40 rows)      101.9 KiB  476.2 KiB    6.24 MiB
  scalar matrix (100 x 1 000 strings)     858.6 KiB   3.08 MiB    4.03 MiB
  JSON blob field (5 000 objects)         232.7 KiB  964.1 KiB  1017.9 KiB
  argument-heavy field (2 000 items)       99.8 KiB  495.1 KiB    6.61 MiB

  The scalar matrix is stored by reference in production (performance §7.5), so its
  store is the caller's arrays; the read then copies every array.

